### What is S3 versioning?

S3 versioning is a bucket-level setting that preserves every version of every object. When enabled, each upload to the same key creates a new current version with a unique version ID, and the previous version becomes noncurrent but is still stored and accessible by its ID.

```java
package com.bank.storage;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.*;
import java.nio.file.Path;

public class VersionedUploader {
    private final S3Client s3 = S3Client.create();

    public String uploadKycDocument(String customerId, Path filePath) {
        PutObjectResponse resp = s3.putObject(
            PutObjectRequest.builder()
                .bucket("nbi-kyc")
                .key(customerId + "/pan-card.pdf")
                .build(),
            filePath);
        String versionId = resp.versionId(); // e.g. "3HL4kqtJ..."
        System.out.println("Uploaded version: " + versionId);
        return versionId;
    }
}
```

### How do delete markers work?

Deletion behaves specially on a versioned bucket. A simple delete does not remove any data. Instead, S3 writes a delete marker as the new current version, so a normal GET returns a 404 as if the object is gone. The actual data versions still exist underneath. To restore, you delete the delete marker by its version ID, and the previous version becomes current again.

```java
package com.bank.storage;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.*;

public class DeleteMarkerRecovery {
    private final S3Client s3 = S3Client.create();

    public void restoreDeletedObject(String bucket, String key, String deleteMarkerVersionId) {
        // Remove the delete marker to un-hide the object
        s3.deleteObject(DeleteObjectRequest.builder()
            .bucket(bucket)
            .key(key)
            .versionId(deleteMarkerVersionId)
            .build());
        System.out.println("Delete marker removed. Object restored: " + key);
    }

    public void permanentlyDelete(String bucket, String key, String specificVersionId) {
        // This truly erases the version -- deliberate, separate action
        s3.deleteObject(DeleteObjectRequest.builder()
            .bucket(bucket)
            .key(key)
            .versionId(specificVersionId)
            .build());
        System.out.println("Version permanently deleted: " + specificVersionId);
    }
}
```

### How does versioning protect against accidental overwrites?

When someone uploads a wrong file over the correct one, the correct version is retained as a noncurrent version. You can list all versions of an object, identify the correct one by its version ID and timestamp, and either download it directly or make it current again by copying it back with a new PUT.

```java
package com.bank.storage;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.*;
import java.util.List;

public class VersionLister {
    private final S3Client s3 = S3Client.create();

    public void listVersions(String bucket, String key) {
        ListObjectVersionsResponse resp = s3.listObjectVersions(
            ListObjectVersionsRequest.builder()
                .bucket(bucket).prefix(key).build());
        List<ObjectVersion> versions = resp.versions();
        for (ObjectVersion v : versions) {
            System.out.println("Version: " + v.versionId()
                + " | Modified: " + v.lastModified()
                + " | Current: " + v.isLatest());
        }
    }
}
```

### How do lifecycle rules work with versioning?

Keeping every version forever is expensive. Lifecycle rules can target noncurrent versions specifically: transition noncurrent versions to a cheaper class after a number of days, and permanently expire noncurrent versions after a safe period, while the current version stays untouched. Lifecycle rules can also clean up expired delete markers and incomplete multipart uploads.

```java
// Lifecycle rule targeting noncurrent versions
package com.bank.storage;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.*;
import java.util.List;

public class NoncurrentVersionLifecycle {
    public static LifecycleRule buildNoncurrentRule() {
        return LifecycleRule.builder()
            .id("expire-old-kyc-versions")
            .filter(LifecycleRuleFilter.builder().prefix("kyc/").build())
            .status(ExpirationStatus.ENABLED)
            .noncurrentVersionTransitions(List.of(
                NoncurrentVersionTransition.builder()
                    .noncurrentDays(30)
                    .storageClass(TransitionStorageClass.STANDARD_IA)
                    .build()))
            .noncurrentVersionExpiration(
                NoncurrentVersionExpiration.builder()
                    .noncurrentDays(90)
                    .build())
            .build();
    }
}
```

### What is MFA Delete?

MFA Delete adds a strong safeguard. When enabled, two sensitive operations require a multi-factor authentication code from a hardware or virtual device: permanently deleting a specific object version, and disabling or suspending versioning on the bucket. This stops even a compromised set of credentials from wiping version history.

```bash
# MFA Delete can only be set by the root account via CLI
aws s3api put-bucket-versioning \
  --bucket nbi-kyc \
  --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled,MFADelete=Enabled \
  --mfa "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:mfa/root-device 123456"
```

```java
// MFA Delete prevents programmatic mass deletion
package com.bank.storage;

public class MfaDeleteGuard {
    public static void explain() {
        System.out.println("With MFA Delete enabled:");
        System.out.println("- Deleting a specific version ID requires an MFA code");
        System.out.println("- Disabling versioning requires an MFA code");
        System.out.println("- Even compromised admin credentials cannot wipe history");
    }
}
```

### What is Object Lock and how does it compare to MFA Delete?

Object Lock provides write-once-read-many (WORM) compliance. In compliance mode, no one, not even the root account, can delete a locked object before its retention period expires. In governance mode, users with special permissions can override the lock. Object Lock is preferred for regulatory compliance because it is stricter than MFA Delete, which only adds a second factor rather than a hard block.

```java
package com.bank.storage;

public class RetentionComparison {
    public static void compare() {
        System.out.println("MFA Delete: requires MFA code to delete a version");
        System.out.println("  - Root account can still delete with the code");
        System.out.println("  - Can be disabled by root with MFA");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Object Lock (Compliance mode): nobody can delete");
        System.out.println("  - Not even root can override until retention expires");
        System.out.println("  - Required for regulatory WORM compliance");
    }
}
```

### What are the rules and constraints?

Versioning cannot be turned off once enabled, only suspended. Suspending keeps existing versions intact but stops creating new ones for future uploads. Versioning must be enabled before Object Lock can be used. Delete markers are zero-size objects that consume no storage but do count as objects. Each noncurrent version occupies its full size in storage, so versioning can significantly increase storage costs without lifecycle management.

```java
package com.bank.storage;

public class VersioningRules {
    public static void printRules() {
        System.out.println("1. Versioning cannot be disabled, only suspended");
        System.out.println("2. Suspended: existing versions kept, no new ones created");
        System.out.println("3. Delete markers are zero-size but count as objects");
        System.out.println("4. Each noncurrent version occupies full storage");
        System.out.println("5. Object Lock requires versioning to be enabled first");
        System.out.println("6. MFA Delete only manageable by root via CLI/API");
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is enabling versioning without a noncurrent-version lifecycle rule, so every overwrite accumulates hidden versions that silently inflate the storage bill. A second mistake is assuming a simple delete actually removes data. A third is relying on MFA Delete alone for compliance when Object Lock is the correct tool.

```java
// Wrong: versioning enabled, no lifecycle rule
// 1000 objects overwritten daily for a year = 365,000 noncurrent versions
// All in Standard, paying full price for data nobody needs

// Correct: versioning + noncurrent version lifecycle rule
// Noncurrent versions transition to Standard-IA after 30 days
// Noncurrent versions expire after 90 days
// Cost controlled, recoverability preserved for 90 days
```
